# Exploratory testing for the control-plane API.
#
# Prefer a UI? The same surface is browsable at http://localhost:5199/scalar (Development only)
# — paste a token from §0 into its auth box. This file stays the scriptable version.
#
# Format: VS Code "REST Client" (humao.rest-client) / JetBrains HTTP Client. Send a request by
# clicking "Send Request" above it. Run them roughly top to bottom the first time — the write
# examples build on each other (onboard -> check -> promote -> offboard).
#
# Prerequisites:
#   dotnet run --project src/AppHost/AppHost.csproj
# Both ports below are pinned in AppHost.cs, so this file works against a fresh run as-is. If
# you have an older stack up, restart it — a run that predates the control plane has nothing
# listening on 5199.
#
# See docs/control-plane.md for what each rule is protecting.

@api = http://localhost:5199
@idp = http://localhost:18080

# --- Identities -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seeded in allowlist/rulesets.json. The mock IdP mints a token for ANY appid you ask for; that
# is a local-only shortcut standing in for the platform guarantee in Azure, and it is exactly
# what makes the negative cases below testable.

@pipelineId = 22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222
# registry-wide onboard; update/offboard scoped to the sample-app ruleset
@platformId = 99999999-9999-9999-9999-999999999999
# unscoped: every verb on every ruleset
@workloadId = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
# a GOVERNED subject (sample-app) — must never be able to write
@strangerId = 44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444
# holds no grant at all
@subjectId  = 33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333
# the workload the "payments" ruleset below will govern

### 0a. Get a token for the pipeline identity. Re-send when it expires (TTL is 5 minutes).
# @name pipelineToken
GET {{idp}}/token?appid={{pipelineId}}

### 0b. Tokens for the other identities, used by the negative cases further down.
# @name platformToken
GET {{idp}}/token?appid={{platformId}}

###
# @name workloadToken
GET {{idp}}/token?appid={{workloadId}}

###
# @name strangerToken
GET {{idp}}/token?appid={{strangerId}}

# If your client does not resolve these request variables, paste a token in by hand:
#   @pipeline = eyJhbGciOi...
@pipeline = {{pipelineToken.response.body.*}}
@platform = {{platformToken.response.body.*}}
@workload = {{workloadToken.response.body.*}}
@stranger = {{strangerToken.response.body.*}}


# ==============================================================================================
# Authentication — the token is validated exactly as the proxy validates workload tokens
# ==============================================================================================

### 1a. No token at all -> 401
GET {{api}}/rulesets

### 1b. A token that is not a JWT -> 401
GET {{api}}/rulesets
Authorization: Bearer not-a-jwt

### 1c. A well-formed token from the wrong issuer -> 401 (signature/issuer mismatch)
GET {{api}}/rulesets
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJhcHBpZCI6IjIyMjIyMjIyLTIyMjItMjIyMi0yMjIyLTIyMjIyMjIyMjIyMiIsImlzcyI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZpbC5leGFtcGxlLyIsImF1ZCI6ImVncmVzcy1wcm94eSIsImV4cCI6NDEwMjQ0NDgwMH0.Ck7g0DYP_dQnQ4dTQmgVXeQdlqB6MyDgVOTn2mUZ0Yc

### 1d. Liveness (only mapped in Development, which is how Aspire runs it)
GET {{api}}/health


# ==============================================================================================
# Reads are open — any valid token sees the whole egress posture, whatever it may write
# ==============================================================================================

### 2a. List every ruleset, as an identity holding NO grants
GET {{api}}/rulesets
Authorization: Bearer {{stranger}}

### 2b. Read one ruleset
GET {{api}}/rulesets/sample-app
Authorization: Bearer {{stranger}}

### 2c. Unknown ruleset -> 404
GET {{api}}/rulesets/does-not-exist
Authorization: Bearer {{stranger}}


# ==============================================================================================
# The loop: onboard -> check -> promote -> offboard
# ==============================================================================================

### 3. Onboard "payments" WITHOUT an action.
# -> 201 with "action":"report" — the on-ramp default for a workload whose egress you do not
#    yet know. Also note "owner": the caller is recorded trust-on-first-use and now holds
#    update/offboard on it without any platform grant.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "{{subjectId}}" }],
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"]
  }
}

### 3b. ...and an explicit action IS honoured at onboard. report is a default, not an override:
# it passes all traffic and only logs, so forcing it over an explicit enforce would grant a new
# workload more egress than it asked for. (Offboard payments first to re-run this from scratch.)
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments-strict
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "aaaaaaaa-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeeeeeeeeeee" }],
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 4. Dry-run the next push. Returns the {added, removed} diff and writes NOTHING —
# this is what a pipeline gates on before pushing.
POST {{api}}/rulesets/payments:check
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com", "api.vendor.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 5. Confirm the dry run really wrote nothing (still one host, still report)
GET {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}

### 6. Promote to enforce, once the report-mode logs are clean. Authorized by ownership.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 7. Widen an ALREADY ENFORCING ruleset.
# -> stays "enforce": adding a host never downgrades rules already in force. The addition is
#    audited instead — look for `host ... ADDED to ruleset payments by ...` in the API log.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com", "api.vendor.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 8. Full replace removes what the push omits -> "removed":["api.vendor.com"], audited.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 9. A desired-state pipeline may restate the stored subjects unchanged (no-op, not an error)
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "{{subjectId}}" }],
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}


# ==============================================================================================
# The rules worth poking at — every one of these should be refused
# ==============================================================================================

### 10. Writer != subject: a GOVERNED workload tries to widen its own ruleset -> 403.
# This is the attack the proxy exists to stop. It fails even though this identity holds
# grants — being a subject disqualifies it outright.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/sample-app
Authorization: Bearer {{workload}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.github.com", "exfil.example.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 11. No grant, no write -> 403
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{stranger}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["evil.example.com"], "action": "enforce" }
}

### 12. Onboard without the verb -> 403, and nothing is created
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/squatted
Authorization: Bearer {{stranger}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "77777777-7777-7777-7777-777777777777" }],
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["evil.example.com"] }
}

### 13. Subjects are frozen after onboard -> 400.
# Steady-state identity hijack is impossible: an update can never move a different workload
# under these rules.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "66666666-6666-6666-6666-666666666666" }],
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"], "action": "enforce" }
}

### 14. One subject, one ruleset -> 409. First-come protects an onboarded subject from
# being claimed by someone else's ruleset.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments-copy
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "{{subjectId}}" }],
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"] }
}

### 15. allowed_hosts holds exact FQDNs — no URLs -> 400
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["https://api.stripe.com"], "action": "enforce" }
}

### 16. ...no wildcards -> 400
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["*.stripe.com"], "action": "enforce" }
}

### 17. ...and no bare IPs -> 400
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["10.0.0.1"], "action": "enforce" }
}

### 18. Unknown action -> 400
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"], "action": "allow-everything" }
}

### 19. Ruleset names are lowercase slugs -> 400
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/Payments_v2
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [{ "appid": "88888888-8888-8888-8888-888888888888" }],
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"] }
}


# ==============================================================================================
# Multi-subject rulesets and the platform identity
# ==============================================================================================

### 20. A ruleset governs one or more clients. This one takes an appid AND a source CIDR;
# it renders to one allowlist entry per subject, both carrying the same content.
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/billing
Authorization: Bearer {{platform}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjects": [
    { "appid": "aaaaaaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa" },
    { "netid": "10.2.0.0/23" }
  ],
  "content": {
    "allowed_hosts": ["api.billing.example.com"],
    "action": "enforce"
  }
}

### 21. The unscoped platform grant may write any ruleset, including one it does not own
PUT {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{platform}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": { "allowed_hosts": ["api.stripe.com"], "action": "enforce" }
}


# ==============================================================================================
# Offboard
# ==============================================================================================

### 22. Offboard -> the subjects are freed and fall to the fallback/deny block on the next
# proxy reload. Decommission is fail-closed by construction.
DELETE {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}

### 23. Offboarding it again -> 404
DELETE {{api}}/rulesets/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{pipeline}}

### 24. Clean up the multi-subject ruleset too
DELETE {{api}}/rulesets/billing
Authorization: Bearer {{platform}}

### 25. Final state — back to just the seeded sample-app ruleset
GET {{api}}/rulesets
Authorization: Bearer {{platform}}
